  "id": "Compound::DB01136 - Gene::146",
  "verbalization": "Carvedilol, a commonly used antihypertensive drug, is known to bind to the Adra1a and Adra2c receptors, which are part of the alpha-1 adrenergic receptor family. These receptors are involved in the regulation of blood pressure and vascular tone. The evidence suggests that Carvedilol exerts its effects through a mechanism involving the alpha-1 adrenergic receptor activity, which is shared by Adra1d. This indicates that Carvedilol may modulate the activity of Adra1d through a common pathway, likely by inhibiting the alpha-1 adrenergic receptor. The integration of multiple paths highlights a consistent relationship between Carvedilol and Adra1d, with the shared involvement of alpha-1 adrenergic receptor activity as a critical mediator. The observed scores support a mechanistic link, though the exact nature of the interaction remains to be fully elucidated.",
